# Messages for shipping.dispatches, the topic that is deliberately fragile.
#
# Its `min.insync.replicas` equals its replication factor, so all three copies of a partition must
# acknowledge every write. Stop one broker of the production cluster and this topic — and only
# this topic — starts refusing writes with NOT_ENOUGH_REPLICAS, while everything else carries on
# with two in-sync replicas out of three. That is the failure the demo exists to show, and it is
# a real Kafka behaviour rather than something staged.
#
# The records themselves continue the story the other topics tell: ORD-10246 was placed, paid and
# then shipped, and its tracking number here is the same one that appears in orders.v1.
#
# Every line is: headers \t key \t value. The literal two characters \t are the field separator,
# not a real tab, so this file survives being opened in any editor. seed.sh converts them.
# %TS-n% becomes an ISO-8601 UTC timestamp n seconds before the seed runs; %TS+n% one n seconds
# after it, which is how an "estimated delivery" stays in the future.
#mode: headers-key-value

carrier:DPD,event-type:DispatchCreated,warehouse:EU-1\tDSP-55010\t{"dispatchId":"DSP-55010","orderId":"ORD-10246","carrier":"DPD","tracking":"DPD-4471203881","dispatchedAt":"%TS-1750%","from":{"warehouse":"EU-1","country":"PL"},"to":{"country":"GB","city":"Bristol","postcode":"BS1 4DJ"},"parcels":1,"weightKg":6.4,"estimatedDelivery":"%TS+86400%"}
